 "result_text": "B902 no longer flags the metaclass case from the issue. A class that lists `type` as a base is now treated as a metaclass. I checked this with a small flake8 run and didn't run the test suite.\n\nIn a metaclass:\n- **Instance methods:** `__init__(cls, ...)` is accepted, and so are the other `cls`-style names (`klass`, `metacls`). Plain `self` is no longer accepted there.\n- **`__new__`:** the first argument can be `metacls`, `metaclass`, `mcs` or `mcls`.\n\nEverything outside metaclasses is checked as before. In my sample file, `cls` as the first argument of an ordinary method was still reported, and the metaclass `__init__` and `__new__` were not.\n\nDetection only looks at the class's direct bases, so a subclass of an existing metaclass (for example `class Sub(StorageMeta)`) isn't recognised and would still be flagged.\n\nThe change is in `bugbear.py`, in `check_for_b902` and the `B902` name lists.",
